    title: "The Study of 'Words' and 'Pauses'",
    body: "Struggling with poverty and illness, Otaki never stopped sharpening his inner world. If the body wouldn't move, the mind would. He devoted himself to an exhaustive study of \"words\" and \"ma\" (the art of pauses and timing).\n\nHe read a single line of dialogue hundreds of times, pursuing the optimal vocal dynamics, the length of silence, and the timing of breath. While other actors drew audiences with dramatic physicality, Otaki polished the power of \"stillness.\"\n\nThe kyudo principle of \"seisha hicchu\" came alive here. Just as a bow draw must contain no wasted motion, words must contain no waste. He poured his soul into every line and imbued even silence with meaning.\n\nThe apprenticeship was long. Years passed without a breakthrough. As fellow actors won acclaim, Otaki labored on in backstage roles and bit parts. Yet it was that very time that forged the \"weight of his words.\"",
